.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
SSH data types (RFC 4251 section 5): byte, boolean, uint32, uint64, string, mpint and name\-list.
.PP
A gf_reader walks a buffer that is already complete.  That is the right shape for SSH: the binary packet layer (packet.h) delivers whole authenticated payloads, so a message is never parsed from a partial buffer.  Reads are all\-or\-nothing: a read that fails leaves the reader where it was and returns GF_ERR_ENCODING.  Variable\-length values are slices into the caller's buffer; nothing is copied or allocated.
.PP
A gf_writer fills a caller\-supplied buffer.  It records the first overflow and ignores every write after it, so a message can be built with no check after each field and one at the end (gf_wr_status).
.SH "TYPES AND CONSTANTS"
.PP
.nf
typedef struct gf_slice
{
  const uint8_t *data;
  size_t len;
} gf_slice;
.fi
.PP
.nf
typedef struct gf_reader
{
  const uint8_t *p;
  size_t len;			/* Bytes left.  */
} gf_reader;
.fi
.PP
.nf
typedef struct gf_writer
{
  uint8_t *buf;
  size_t cap;
  size_t len;
  int err;
} gf_writer;
.fi
